task: bench-across-processes
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-11
branch: feature/bench-across-processes
worktree: ../okay-wt-bap1
spec: docs/benchmarks.md §20

why: §20 names its own asymmetry and has never been able to remove it.
  okay has a distributed lane across four OS processes (stage 7);
  Flink is a MiniCluster and Spark is `local[4]`, both one JVM. So the
  section can say what okay's distribution costs and cannot say what
  theirs does, and the row a reader most wants — the three engines in
  the same deployment mode — is missing.

  It is missing for a reason that turns out to be false: both engines'
  standalone entry points are on the test classpath already
  (StandaloneSessionClusterEntrypoint, TaskManagerRunner,
  deploy.master.Master, deploy.worker.Worker), so a real multi-process
  cluster needs no distribution tarball, only processes and a jar.

what: each engine run ACROSS PROCESSES on this machine, same job, same
  eleven checksums, and the fixed cost separated from the marginal one
  by §20's own least-squares split — because a cluster's startup is
  seconds and the job is a second, and charging that to the events
  would be a statement about the benchmark rather than the engine.

  okay: the stage 7 road (four workers, one coordinator).
  Flink: a JobManager and TaskManagers as JVMs, the job submitted to
    them, not to a MiniCluster.
  Spark: a Master and Workers as JVMs, `spark://` rather than local[4].

what this is NOT: a cluster. One machine, several processes — the
  network is loopback and the disks are one. That is a fair
  DEPLOYMENT-MODE comparison and not a distributed-systems one, and
  §20 has to say so where the rows are.

predict: the two engines lose more to their own deployment than okay
  does, because their fixed cost is a cluster coming up and okay's is
  two round trips; and their marginal rates move less than their fixed
  ones. The risk is entirely in getting the submissions to work —
  Flink needs the job in a jar it can ship, Spark needs its executors
  to find the classes — and if one of them cannot be made to run
  honestly I will say which and why rather than quietly compare two.
